<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Antoine Lavoisier</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1743">1743</a>–<a href="/years/1794">1794</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Antoine-Laurent de Lavoisier</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Antoine Lavoisier was a French nobleman and chemist central to the 18th-century chemical revolution and made chemistry quantitative. He showed the role of oxygen in combustion against the phlogiston theory, named oxygen in 1778 and recognised hydrogen as an element in 1783, and his precise measurements confirmed the conservation of mass. He wrote the first extensive list of elements, helped reform chemical nomenclature and construct the metric system with his wife Marie-Anne Paulze Lavoisier and set out his chemistry in the Traité élémentaire de chimie (1789). An administrator of the Ferme générale, he was charged with tax fraud during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a> and guillotined in 1794.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/antoine-lavoisier.jpg" alt="Antoine Lavoisier" width="330" height="437" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jacques-Louis David · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:David_-_Portrait_of_Monsieur_Lavoisier_(cropped)2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
